REM ==============================================================
REM  Template_433_RAW.sub
REM
REM  Purpose: a structurally complete 433.92 MHz AM RAW SubGhz
REM  file with documented microsecond timings so you can paste
REM  real capture data into RAW_Data without breaking the format.
REM
REM  Why 433.92 MHz: common ISM band for short-range devices
REM  (weather stations, older garage openers, simple alarm
REM  sensors).  This template is a *structural reference*, not a
REM  captured replay of any specific device.
REM
REM  RAW_Data format (per official docs):
REM    - Comma-separated integers in microseconds.
REM    - Numbers alternate positive (pulse length) /
REM      negative (gap length).
REM    - First value must be positive.
REM    - Last value must be positive.
REM    - Typical lengths: 1-10000 entries for a single button press.
REM
REM  Example structure below is a synthesized "button-down" envelope;
REM  replace with real captured timings when you record a signal.
REM ==============================================================

REM Synthesized example marks (~10 ms press + ~500 ms gap) so the file
REM is parseable but does not reproduce a known replay signal. After
REM recording on a real device, replace RAW_Data with the captured line.

REM Pulse (high)   : 500  us
REM Gap   (low)    : 500  us   (one symbol)
REM Long gap       : 10000 us  (inter-frame separator)
REM
REM Sample frame (synthesized):
REM   500, -500, 500, -500, 500, -10000
REM
REM A real button-down on e.g. an OOK 650bps device typically has
REM 24-64 such pulses; capture with the Flipper, then paste the line.

